  "title": "Erdős Problem #820",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $H(n)$ be the smallest integer $l$ such that there exist $k<l$ with $(k^n-1,l^n-1)=1$.\nIs it true that $H(n)=3$ infinitely often? (That is, $(2^n-1,3^n-1)=1$ infinitely often?)\nEstimate $H(n)$. Is it true that there exists some constant $c>0$ such that, for all $\\epsilon>0$, $ H(n) > \\exp(n^{(c-\\epsilon)/\\log\\log n}) $ for infinitely many $n$ and $ H(n) < \\exp(n^{(c+\\epsilon)/\\log\\log n}) $ for all large enough $n$?\nDoes a similar upper bound hold for the smallest $k$ such that $(k^n-1,2^n-1)=1$?",
    "sha256": "30ec0f0fc9126069adac6ed03ad01c7748f8c5d7f91f88a023d7be76402cf22d",
